    Section(
      title: 'Initial Presentation',
      content:
          'A 60-year-old right-handed male is referred to the cancer rehabilitation clinic 6 weeks after total laryngectomy with bilateral selective neck dissection (levels II-IV) for T3N2aM0 squamous cell carcinoma of the larynx. He communicates via writing on a whiteboard and appears frustrated by the slowness of this method. He has a permanent tracheostomy with a heat-moisture exchanger (HME) in place. He completed concurrent chemoradiation (cisplatin + 70 Gy) prior to surgery and has significant radiation fibrosis of the anterior neck. He reports difficulty swallowing anything thicker than thin liquids, a stiff and painful right shoulder that he cannot raise above shoulder level, and progressive social withdrawal. His wife notes he has stopped attending church and refuses phone calls from friends. He lost 14 kg over the past 4 months and remains dependent on supplemental tube feeds via PEG.',
