    Section(
      title: 'History & Systems Review:',
      content:
          '- **Oncologic History**: Laryngeal SCC, T3N2aM0 (stage IVA). 40 pack-year smoking history, quit 6 months ago at diagnosis. 20-year history of heavy alcohol use, currently abstinent 6 months. Prior treatment: concurrent chemoradiation (cisplatin x 3 cycles, 70 Gy to primary + bilateral neck), then salvage total laryngectomy with bilateral selective neck dissection for persistent disease.\n- **Surgical Details**: Total laryngectomy with permanent end tracheostomy. Primary tracheoesophageal puncture (TEP) placed at time of surgery. Bilateral selective neck dissection levels II-IV with sacrifice of right spinal accessory nerve (intraoperative finding of tumor encasement).\n- **Nutrition**: PEG tube placed pre-operatively. Currently tolerating thin liquids by mouth in small sips. Supplemental tube feeds providing 60% of caloric needs. Weight 68 kg (pre-illness weight 82 kg). Prealbumin 14 mg/dL.\n- **Swallowing**: Frequent coughing with thin liquids, sensation of food \'sticking\' at the base of tongue, avoids all solid foods. No prior modified barium swallow since surgery.\n- **Voice/Communication**: TEP in place but patient has not been trained on use. Currently communicating via whiteboard and text-to-speech phone app.\n- **Right Shoulder**: Progressive difficulty raising right arm since neck dissection. Pain rated 6/10 in right trapezius region and shoulder. Unable to reach overhead.\n- **Psychosocial**: Married, two adult children. Retired long-haul truck driver (CDL holder). Wife is primary caregiver. Reports depressed mood, anhedonia, poor sleep. PHQ-9 score 18 (moderately severe depression).\n- **Medications**: Gabapentin 300 mg TID (radiation neuropathy), omeprazole 40 mg daily, metoclopramide 10 mg QID, oxycodone 5 mg Q6H PRN, multivitamin, nicotine patch 14 mg\n- **Review of Systems**: Xerostomia, thick saliva, trismus (interincisal opening 22 mm), hypothyroidism (on levothyroxine since radiation), hearing loss bilateral (cisplatin ototoxicity)',
