    Section(
      title: '+ Describe the swallowing evaluation approach in a post-laryngectomy patient. What are the key risks, and how does swallowing physiology differ from pre-laryngectomy anatomy?',
      content:
          '**Post-Laryngectomy Swallowing Physiology**:\n\nAfter total laryngectomy, the airway and digestive tracts are **permanently separated**. The trachea is sutured to the skin as a permanent stoma, and the pharynx is reconstructed as a closed tube (neopharynx) connecting the oropharynx to the esophagus. This means:\n- **Aspiration risk is eliminated** in an intact laryngectomy (no connection between airway and food passage)\n- Dysphagia is caused by **stricture, fibrosis, or impaired pharyngeal propulsion** rather than airway protection failure\n- The reconstructed neopharynx may have altered compliance and peristalsis\n\n**Swallowing Evaluation Approach**:\n1. **Modified barium swallow (MBS)**: Assess neopharyngeal transit, detect stricture at the pharyngoesophageal (PE) segment, evaluate base-of-tongue retraction (often impaired after radiation), and identify pooling or residue.\n2. **Esophagram**: Evaluate for distal esophageal stricture if symptoms suggest obstruction below the PE segment.\n3. **Fiberoptic endoscopic evaluation**: Via the stoma, can visualize the neopharynx and PE segment from above.\n\n**Key Risks and Complications**:\n1. **Pharyngocutaneous fistula**: Abnormal communication between the neopharynx and skin — presents as saliva or food leaking through the neck wound. Risk factors include prior radiation, malnutrition (low prealbumin as in this patient), diabetes, and hypothyroidism. **This is the most feared early complication** — requires NPO status, wound care, and possible surgical repair.\n2. **Pharyngoesophageal stricture**: Narrowing at the reconstruction site. Common after chemoradiation + surgery. Presents as progressive solid food dysphagia. May require serial dilation.\n3. **Radiation fibrosis**: Causes reduced pharyngeal contraction, base-of-tongue retropulsion weakness, and impaired PE segment opening. Progressive and may worsen for months to years after radiation.\n4. **Pseudo-epiglottic fold**: Tissue fold at the base of tongue that can impede bolus transit.\n\n**This Patient\'s Dysphagia Workup**:\n- MBS to evaluate neopharyngeal transit and PE segment patency\n- Assess base-of-tongue driving force (likely weakened from radiation)\n- Evaluate for stricture requiring dilation\n- Nutritional optimization (prealbumin 14 is suboptimal — target >20)\n- Trismus management to improve oral preparatory phase (interincisal opening only 22 mm)',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ The patient\'s TEP prosthesis is in place but he has not been trained. Describe the three main alaryngeal speech options, their mechanisms, and candidacy factors.',
      content:
          '**Alaryngeal Speech Options After Total Laryngectomy**:\n\n**1. Tracheoesophageal Puncture (TEP) Speech** — Gold Standard:\n- **Mechanism**: A one-way valve prosthesis is placed in a surgically created fistula between the posterior tracheal wall and the anterior esophageal wall. When the patient occludes the stoma (with a finger or hands-free valve), exhaled air is shunted through the prosthesis into the esophagus and vibrates the PE segment (neovibratory source), producing voice.\n- **Advantages**: Most natural-sounding voice, highest speech intelligibility (80-90%), fastest rehabilitation, adequate volume and phrase length.\n- **Candidacy**: Requires adequate pulmonary function to generate airflow, intact PE segment vibration (no spasm or hypotonicity), manual dexterity to occlude stoma (or hands-free valve), and motivation for prosthesis care.\n- **Maintenance**: Prosthesis requires replacement every 3-6 months due to candidal colonization. Patient or caregiver must learn cleaning and troubleshooting.\n- **Complications**: Prosthesis leakage (through or around), candidal colonization, granulation tissue, aspiration through the puncture site if prosthesis dislodges.\n\n**2. Electrolarynx (Artificial Larynx)**:\n- **Mechanism**: A battery-powered handheld device that generates vibration. Placed against the neck or cheek, the vibrations are transmitted through tissue into the oral cavity. The patient articulates normally to shape the sound into speech.\n- **Advantages**: Easy to learn, immediately available, no surgical procedure needed, inexpensive.\n- **Disadvantages**: Mechanical, robotic voice quality; requires one hand to hold device (limits function); can be difficult to use with radiation fibrosis (poor sound transmission through fibrotic tissue — a significant issue for this patient); reduced intelligibility in noisy environments.\n- **Candidacy**: Good option for immediate communication while learning TEP speech, or as backup when TEP is non-functional.\n\n**3. Esophageal Speech**:\n- **Mechanism**: The patient learns to insufflate air into the upper esophagus and then release it in a controlled belch. The vibrating PE segment produces sound that is articulated into speech.\n- **Advantages**: No device or prosthesis needed, both hands free.\n- **Disadvantages**: Most difficult to learn (only 25-30% achieve functional fluency), quiet voice, short phrase length (4-5 syllables per air charge), long learning curve (months to years).\n- **Candidacy**: Intact PE segment, strong motivation, adequate cognitive function, good SLP support.\n\n**Recommendation for This Patient**:\n- **Primary**: TEP speech training — prosthesis already in place. Refer to SLP experienced in alaryngeal speech rehabilitation. Start with digital stoma occlusion technique, progress to hands-free HME valve if successful.\n- **Interim**: Electrolarynx for immediate communication needs. May have reduced effectiveness due to radiation fibrosis — try intraoral adapter if neck placement is ineffective.\n- **Backup**: Text-to-speech apps on smartphone/tablet for complex communication needs.',
    ),
    Section(
