    Section(
      title: '+ Develop a comprehensive rehabilitation plan addressing this patient\'s swallowing, shoulder dysfunction, radiation fibrosis, and nutritional needs.',
      content:
          '**1. Swallowing Rehabilitation (SLP-Directed)**:\n- **Tongue base exercises**: Effortful swallow, Masako (tongue-hold) maneuver, and tongue base retraction exercises to strengthen propulsive force.\n- **Mendelsohn maneuver**: To improve PE segment opening duration and bolus transit.\n- **Diet progression**: Advance from thin liquids to nectar-thick, then purees as residue decreases. Track percentage of oral intake versus tube feeds weekly.\n- **PEG tube management**: Maintain PEG for supplemental feeds until patient consistently meets >75% of caloric needs orally. Do NOT remove prematurely.\n- **Oral care**: Aggressive xerostomia management — saliva substitutes, frequent water sips, pilocarpine 5 mg TID if tolerated. Fluoride trays for dental protection.\n\n**2. Trismus Management**:\n- **Therabite or Dynasplint jaw device**: Progressive passive stretch protocol 3 times daily for 5-10 minutes. Goal: interincisal opening >35 mm.\n- **Tongue depressor stacking**: Simple home exercise supplement.\n- **Monitor**: Weekly measurements of interincisal distance.\n\n**3. Right Shoulder — Spinal Accessory Nerve Palsy Rehabilitation**:\n- **Phase 1 (Current — 6 weeks post-op)**: PROM to maintain glenohumeral and scapulothoracic mobility. Scapular stabilization exercises within pain-free range. Pendulum exercises. Ice and NSAID for pain management.\n- **Phase 2 (8-12 weeks)**: Active-assisted and active scapular retraction, protraction, elevation. Serratus anterior and rhomboid strengthening to compensate for trapezius weakness. Progressive resistance band exercises.\n- **Phase 3 (3-6 months)**: If no accessory nerve recovery, focus on compensatory strengthening of levator scapulae, rhomboids, serratus anterior. Consider EMG at 3-4 months to assess reinnervation potential.\n- **Surgical option**: If no nerve recovery by 9-12 months, consider Eden-Lange muscle transfer (transfer of levator scapulae and rhomboids to replace trapezius function) or nerve grafting if identified early.\n- **Pain management**: Gabapentin continuation, topical lidocaine patches to trapezius region, trigger point injections PRN.\n\n**4. Radiation Fibrosis Syndrome Management**:\n- **Manual therapy**: Myofascial release, soft tissue mobilization of fibrotic neck tissues by therapist trained in oncology rehabilitation.\n- **Cervical ROM exercises**: Gentle active and passive neck ROM 3-4 times daily.\n- **Pentoxifylline 400 mg TID + Vitamin E 1000 IU daily**: Evidence-based combination for radiation fibrosis (PENTOCLO protocol). Discuss with oncologist.\n- **Hyperbaric oxygen therapy**: Consider referral if fibrosis is progressive and refractory to conservative measures.\n\n**5. Nutritional Optimization**:\n- **Caloric goal**: 30-35 kcal/kg/day (2040-2380 kcal/day) with 1.5 g protein/kg/day.\n- **Dietitian-guided plan**: Optimize oral intake with calorie-dense, easy-to-swallow foods (smoothies, protein shakes, soups). Supplement remainder via PEG.\n- **Monitor**: Weekly weights, monthly prealbumin (goal >20 mg/dL).\n\n**6. Depression Management**:\n- PHQ-9 of 18 warrants treatment. Start SSRI (sertraline 50 mg daily — avoid duloxetine with hepatic concerns given alcohol history).\n- Psycho-oncology referral for adjustment disorder with depressed mood.\n- Screen for suicidal ideation (head/neck cancer patients have among the highest suicide rates of all cancer diagnoses).',
    ),
