    Section(
      title: '+ Describe the multidisciplinary coordination model for post-laryngectomy rehabilitation, insurance considerations for communication devices, and smoking cessation integration.',
      content:
          '**Multidisciplinary Team Coordination**:\n1. **Speech-Language Pathologist (SLP)**: Central role — TEP speech training, swallowing therapy, trismus exercises, electrolarynx fitting. Should be SLP with **head and neck cancer specialization**. Frequency: 2-3 sessions/week initially.\n2. **Physiatrist (PM&R)**: Overall rehabilitation plan coordination, shoulder dysfunction management, radiation fibrosis treatment, medication management, functional outcome tracking.\n3. **Head & Neck Surgeon/ENT**: Ongoing cancer surveillance (every 2-3 months year 1), TEP prosthesis replacement, stricture management, fistula monitoring.\n4. **Medical Oncologist**: Surveillance imaging, management of treatment sequelae.\n5. **Radiation Oncologist**: Monitoring radiation late effects (fibrosis, hypothyroidism, carotid stenosis screening).\n6. **Dietitian**: Nutritional optimization, PEG feed management, oral diet advancement guidance.\n7. **Psychologist/Psychiatrist**: Depression treatment, adjustment counseling, body image support.\n8. **Physical Therapist**: Shoulder rehabilitation, cervical ROM, general deconditioning.\n9. **Audiologist**: Cisplatin ototoxicity monitoring, hearing aid evaluation.\n\n**Cancer Survivorship Program**: Integrate into a formal survivorship clinic with a survivorship care plan documenting all treatments received, ongoing side effects, surveillance schedule, and rehabilitation needs.\n\n**Insurance and Device Coverage**:\n- **TEP prosthesis**: Covered as a prosthetic device under most insurance plans (Medicare Part B covers as DME). Replacement prostheses typically covered every 3-6 months.\n- **Electrolarynx**: Covered as a speech-generating device under Medicare and most commercial plans. Requires SLP prescription and documentation of medical necessity.\n- **HME/hands-free speaking valves**: Often require prior authorization. Document medical necessity (pulmonary hygiene, hands-free communication for safety).\n- **Speech therapy**: Covered under rehabilitation benefits. May require pre-authorization. Document specific alaryngeal speech rehabilitation goals.\n- **Therabite/trismus device**: Often covered under DME benefit with documentation of radiation-induced trismus.\n\n**Smoking Cessation Integration**:\n- **Current status**: Abstinent 6 months on nicotine patch, but reporting cravings.\n- **Continue pharmacotherapy**: Nicotine patch taper. Consider adding nicotine lozenge for breakthrough cravings. Discuss varenicline or bupropion if cravings persist.\n- **Behavioral counseling**: Refer to tobacco cessation counselor or quitline (1-800-QUIT-NOW). Cognitive behavioral techniques for craving management.\n- **Motivation**: Continued smoking after laryngectomy increases risk of stomal recurrence, second primary cancers, and impaired wound healing. Frame cessation as integral to rehabilitation success.\n- **Support group**: Laryngectomee support groups often reinforce cessation (International Association of Laryngectomees, WebWhispers online community).',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          'During the rehabilitation evaluation, the patient writes on his whiteboard: \'Nobody will understand me anymore. I can\'t talk, I can\'t eat, I can barely move my arm. I\'m useless to my family. What is the point of all this rehab?\' His wife, sitting beside him, begins to cry.',
    ),
    Section(
      title: '+ How would you address this patient\'s profound sense of loss and hopelessness while also supporting his wife as caregiver?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**1. Create space for the grief and validate both patient and caregiver**:\n\'I hear you, and I want you to know that what you are feeling makes complete sense. You have been through an enormous ordeal — losing your natural voice, struggling to eat, dealing with pain and physical changes. These are real losses, and you have every right to grieve them. [Turning to wife] And I can see how much this is affecting you too. You are carrying a heavy burden as well.\'\n\n**2. Acknowledge the voice loss specifically — it is an identity loss**:\n\'Your voice was part of who you are. It is how you connected with your wife, your children, your friends. Losing it is not just a physical change — it is a loss of something deeply personal. I do not want to minimize that.\'\n\n**3. Offer realistic hope without false promises**:\n\'What I can tell you is that you are only six weeks out from surgery, and you have a tracheoesophageal prosthesis already in place. Many patients who feel exactly the way you feel right now go on to communicate very effectively with TEP speech. It sounds different from your original voice, but most people who learn it say they can have normal conversations — on the phone, at dinner, even in groups. I have seen patients return to social lives they thought were over.\'\n\n**4. Reframe "useless" with concrete evidence**:\n\'You said you are useless to your family. But your wife is here beside you right now, and she is here because you matter to her — not because of what you can do physically, but because of who you are. The fact that you showed up to this appointment and are engaging in this conversation tells me you still have fight in you, even when it does not feel that way.\'\n\n**5. Address the suicidality screen directly**:\n\'When you say, "what is the point," I want to make sure I understand what you mean. Are you having thoughts of not wanting to be here, or of harming yourself?\' [Screen for active suicidal ideation. Head and neck cancer patients have elevated suicide risk. If positive, escalate immediately with psychiatry consultation and safety planning.]\n\n**6. Create a tangible near-term goal**:\n\'Rather than thinking about everything at once, I would like to focus on one thing together this week. Let us start speech therapy so you can try your TEP voice. If we can get you speaking again — even a few words — I think it will change how you feel about everything else. Would you be willing to try that?\'\n\n**7. Support the caregiver**:\n\'[To wife] I also want to make sure you have support. Caregiving for someone after laryngectomy is exhausting, and it is not something you should do alone. We have a social worker and a caregiver support group I would like to connect you with. Your wellbeing matters too — you cannot pour from an empty cup.\'\n\n**Key principles demonstrated**: Grief validation for both patient and caregiver, direct screening for suicidal ideation in a high-risk population, realistic hope grounded in evidence, reframing self-worth, concrete goal-setting for agency, caregiver support integration.',
    ),
  ],
